text:
Protecting the deciders’ review
Rerunning the script rewrites the mapping file. If the deciders have already reviewed any code in it, the script stops before calling any model, so their review cannot be overwritten; the existing file has to be renamed or moved first, and the reviewed rows copied into the new one.


Click to show/hide R Code
# A rerun rewrites the mapping file. Stop before any API call if the deciders
# have already reviewed codes in it, so their review is not overwritten.
if (file.exists(esrd_cause_mapping_file)) {
  existing_mapping &lt;- read_csv(
    esrd_cause_mapping_file,
    col_types = cols(.default = col_character())
  )
  # Files written before the `reviewer` column held the deciders' initials in
  # `reviewed_by`, beside the models' "unanimous" and "2 of 3 models"
  reviewed_codes &lt;- if ("reviewer" %in% names(existing_mapping)) {
    filter(existing_mapping, !is.na(reviewer))
  } else {
    filter(
      existing_mapping,
      !is.na(reviewed_by),
      !str_detect(reviewed_by, "^unanimous$|of \\d+ models$")
    )
  }

  if (nrow(reviewed_codes) &gt; 0) {
    stop(str_glue(
      "{esrd_cause_mapping_file} has {nrow(reviewed_codes)} code(s) reviewed by the deciders. ",
      "Rename or move it before rerunning, then copy the reviewed rows into the new file."
    ), call. = FALSE)
  }
}

The code list
The original mapping spreadsheet is lost. Its replacement starts from a public list: every code of USRDS format $PD96DET with its description, from Appendix C of the Researcher’s Guide (United States Renal Data System 2024), which usRds already holds. There are 445 codes: 128 ICD-10-CM codes from the 2015 form, and 317 older codes, mostly ICD-9-CM codes (many with a letter added by USRDS) plus a few that mean the cause is missing. No patient data are involved, and the list is not filtered by what appears in the data. (The original spreadsheet was built from the codes that appeared in the data.) The script stops if a code is listed twice.


Click to show/hide R Code
# usRds holds Appendix C internally; usRds:::metadata_c is used until the
# package exports the formats (to be raised in the usRds project). The
# **OTHER** row is the format's catch-all for unlisted values, not a code.
cause_codes &lt;- usRds:::metadata_c[["PD96DET"]] |&gt;
  transmute(
    code = str_trim(as.character(value)),
    description = str_squish(as.character(description))
  ) |&gt;
  filter(code != "**OTHER**")

# A code listed twice would be classified twice; stop rather than keep one
duplicated_codes &lt;- cause_codes |&gt;
  count(code) |&gt;
  filter(n &gt; 1)

if (nrow(duplicated_codes) &gt; 0) {
  stop(str_glue(
    "$PD96DET lists {nrow(duplicated_codes)} code(s) more than once: ",
    "{str_flatten_comma(duplicated_codes$code)}."
  ), call. = FALSE)
}

Prompt and schema in code
The structured output schema restricts each answer to the six category names. The check in this section confirms that the prompt defines each of them. Each saved answer file carries a short fingerprint of the prompt text, so an edited prompt is sent to every provider again; the fingerprint is also recorded in the mapping file.


Click to show/hide R Code
# The prompt is the text file esrd_classifier_prompt_file (prompts/), shown in
# full on the website. It defines each category in words; {{code_list}} is
# filled in for each batch.
prompt_text &lt;- read_lines(esrd_classifier_prompt_file)

# The structured output can only return names from esrd_cause_categories, so
# the prompt must define each of them under exactly that name
undefined_categories &lt;- esrd_cause_categories |&gt;
  discard(\(category) any(str_starts(prompt_text, str_glue("- {category}:"))))

if (length(undefined_categories) &gt; 0) {
  stop(str_glue(
    "{esrd_classifier_prompt_file} has no definition line ",
    "(\"- &lt;category&gt;: ...\") for: {str_flatten_comma(undefined_categories)}."
  ), call. = FALSE)
}

classification_prompt &lt;- function(batch) {
  ellmer::interpolate_file(
    esrd_classifier_prompt_file,
    code_list = str_flatten(str_c(batch$code, ": ", batch$description), "\n")
  )
}

# A short fingerprint of the prompt text, ignoring Windows line endings. It is
# part of each saved answer file's name, so editing the prompt asks every
# provider again instead of reusing answers to the old prompt.
prompt_version &lt;- prompt_text |&gt;
  str_remove_all("\r") |&gt;
  rlang::hash() |&gt;
  str_sub(1, 8)

classification_type &lt;- ellmer::type_array(
  ellmer::type_object(
    code = ellmer::type_string("The code exactly as given"),
    category = ellmer::type_enum(esrd_cause_categories, "Cause of kidney failure category"),
    reason = ellmer::type_string("Reason, at most 15 words")
  )
)

Running, checking and saving each model
Before asking any model, the script checks that Ollama is running and has the local models that still need to be asked. While it runs, it reports each model, whether its answers are read from a saved file or asked for, each batch as it is sent, and how long the provider took. The voting models’ answers are checked on every run, whether they are new or read back from a saved file: every code answered exactly once, no invented codes, and only the six categories (check_votes(), on the functions page). The local models’ answers are kept as they came back, and are counted rather than checked (below). Local models get up to 30 minutes per answer (esrd_classifier_timeout_s); ellmer’s default of 5 minutes was too short on a laptop processor. Each batch’s answers are saved as soon as the batch finishes, in a partial file beside the provider’s answer file, so a rerun after a failure (a timeout, a daily quota, a computer going to sleep) asks only the codes that provider has not yet answered. When a provider finishes, its answers are saved under the provider, model and prompt version, and the partial file is deleted; a rerun then asks only the providers that have not finished. A saved file that no longer matches the code list, for example after a usRds update, stops the script; deleting that file asks the provider again.
Functions used: classify_codes(), check_votes(), cached_votes().


Click to show/hide R Code
# Each model's answers are saved in esrd_votes_dir under the provider, model
# and prompt version, so a rerun after a failure asks only the models with no
# saved file. The voting models' answers are checked again on every run
# (check_votes()), so a file that no longer matches the code list, for
# example after a usRds update, stops the script instead of being used;
# delete that file to ask the model again. The local models' answers are kept
# as they came back and reported (local_model_report()), not checked.
votes_path &lt;- function(provider) {
  # Ollama names contain ":", which Windows does not allow in file names
  model &lt;- str_replace_all(esrd_classifier_models[[provider]], "[^A-Za-z0-9._-]", "-")
  file.path(esrd_votes_dir, str_glue("{provider}-{model}-{prompt_version}.csv"))
}

# Before asking any model, check that Ollama is running and has the local
# models that still need to be asked
local_to_ask &lt;- names(providers) |&gt;
  keep(\(provider) str_starts(provider, "ollama_")) |&gt;
  discard(\(provider) file.exists(votes_path(provider)))

if (length(local_to_ask) &gt; 0) {
  installed &lt;- tryCatch(
    ellmer::models_ollama()$id,
    error = function(e) {
      stop("Ollama is not running on this computer. Start it, then rerun.", call. = FALSE)
    }
  )
  not_pulled &lt;- setdiff(unlist(esrd_classifier_models[local_to_ask]), installed)
  if (length(not_pulled) &gt; 0) {
    stop(str_glue(
      "Ollama does not have {str_flatten_comma(not_pulled)}. ",
      "Run in a terminal: {str_flatten(str_c('ollama pull ', not_pulled), '; ')}"
    ), call. = FALSE)
  }
}

message(str_glue(
  "Classifying {nrow(cause_codes)} codes with {length(providers)} models. ",
  "Saved answers are reused; a model that has to be asked can take a minute ",
  "or more per batch."
))

all_votes &lt;- providers |&gt;
  imap(function(ask, provider) {
    message("\n", provider, " (", esrd_classifier_models[[provider]], ")")
    answers &lt;- cached_votes(
      path = votes_path(provider),
      classify = function(partial_path) {
        classify_codes(
          cause_codes,
          ask,
          batch_size = esrd_classifier_batch_sizes[[provider]],
          label = provider,
          partial_path = partial_path
        )
      }
    )
    if (provider %in% esrd_classifier_voting) {
      check_votes(answers, cause_codes, esrd_cause_categories, provider)
    }
    mutate(answers, provider = provider)
  }) |&gt;
  list_rbind()

# Only the voting models decide the mapping
votes &lt;- filter(all_votes, provider %in% esrd_classifier_voting)

Cut points
The 25th, 50th and 75th percentiles of density among all patients, zeros included, as in the original code. The paper reports 1.36, 1.95 and 2.76 facilities per 100,000. The script stops if the cut points are not three distinct positive values (for example, if a quarter or more of patients lived in HSAs with no facility, the first cut point would be 0 and category 2 would be empty).


Click to show/hide R Code
# Percentiles of density among all patients in the cohort, zeros included,
# as in the original code. The paper reports 1.36, 1.95 and 2.76.
density_cutpoints &lt;- cohort |&gt;
  pull(hsa_center_density) |&gt;
  quantile(probs = density_percentiles) |&gt;
  unname()

# If a quarter or more of patients live in HSAs with no facility, the first cut
# point is 0 and category 2 would be empty; stop rather than report it.
if (first(density_cutpoints) &lt;= 0 || anyDuplicated(density_cutpoints) &gt; 0) {
  stop(str_glue(
    "Density cut points are not distinct and positive: ",
    "{str_flatten_comma(round(density_cutpoints, 2))}"
  ), call. = FALSE)
}

Categories
Zero facilities, then four categories each including its upper bound. The script stops if any patient in the cohort has no category.
Functions used: categorize_density().


Click to show/hide R Code
cohort &lt;- cohort |&gt;
  mutate(density_category = categorize_density(hsa_center_density, density_cutpoints)) |&gt;
  set_variable_labels(density_category = "HSA facility density per 100,000 population (category)")

if (anyNA(cohort$density_category)) {
  stop("Some patients have no density category.", call. = FALSE)
}

Residence on the dialysis date
An interval join picks the residence period that contains the date of dialysis start. A patient with no such period, or with more than one, gets no residence and is excluded in the flowchart, where the count is shown.
Functions used: assign_region().


Click to show/hide R Code
# The residence period that contains the date of dialysis start. Patients with
# no such period, or more than one, get no residence here and are excluded in
# create_cohort.R.
residence_on_dialysis_date &lt;- medevid_forms |&gt;
  filter(first_form) |&gt;
  select(USRDS_ID, DIALDAT) |&gt;
  inner_join(
    residence,
    by = join_by(USRDS_ID, between(DIALDAT, BEGRES, ENDRES)),
    relationship = "one-to-many"
  ) |&gt;
  filter(n() == 1, .by = USRDS_ID) |&gt;
  transmute(USRDS_ID, zip = ZIPCODE, state = str_to_upper(STATE))

medevid_forms &lt;- medevid_forms |&gt;
  left_join(residence_on_dialysis_date, by = "USRDS_ID", relationship = "many-to-one") |&gt;
  mutate(
    region = assign_region(state, census_regions),
    has_residence = !is.na(zip)
  )

# The joins must not add or drop forms
if (nrow(medevid_forms) != nrow(medevid_labelled)) {
  stop("Matching residences changed the number of Medical Evidence forms.", call. = FALSE)
}

Cause of kidney failure mapping
The reviewed mapping, written by Classifying codes, must exist and give every code one of the six categories; otherwise the run stops with instructions. Only code and final_category are read.
Functions used: assert_unique().


Click to show/hide R Code
# The reviewed mapping of every $PD96DET code (R/classify_esrd_causes.R).
if (!file.exists(esrd_cause_mapping_file)) {
  stop(str_glue(
    "Missing {esrd_cause_mapping_file}. Run R/classify_esrd_causes.R and review it first."
  ), call. = FALSE)
}
esrd_cause_mapping &lt;- esrd_cause_mapping_file |&gt;
  read_csv(col_types = cols(.default = col_character())) |&gt;
  transmute(PDIS = code, esrd_cause = final_category) |&gt;
  assert_unique(PDIS)

unreviewed_codes &lt;- esrd_cause_mapping |&gt;
  filter(!esrd_cause %in% esrd_cause_categories)

if (nrow(unreviewed_codes) &gt; 0) {
  stop(str_glue(
    "{nrow(unreviewed_codes)} code(s) in {esrd_cause_mapping_file} have no final_category ",
    "from esrd_cause_categories (setup.R)."
  ), call. = FALSE)
}


A first form whose cause code is blank, or is not in the mapping, counts as Other, as in the original code. A blank cause is expected; a code that is filled in but missing from the mapping is not, and many of them would suggest that the codes are written differently in the Medical Evidence file and in the code list (case, spaces, leading zeros). That would move real causes into Other without any error, so the script warns with the share of first forms affected.


Click to show/hide R Code
# A first form whose cause code is filled in but not in the mapping is counted
# as Other below. Many such forms would suggest the codes are written
# differently in MEDEVID and in $PD96DET (case, spaces, leading zeros), which
# would move real causes into Other, so warn with the share affected.
cause_coverage &lt;- medevid_forms |&gt;
  filter(first_form, !is.na(PDIS), PDIS != "") |&gt;
  summarize(
    forms = n(),
    unmapped = sum(!PDIS %in% esrd_cause_mapping$PDIS)
  )

if (cause_coverage$unmapped &gt; 0) {
  warning(str_glue(
    "{round(100 * cause_coverage$unmapped / cause_coverage$forms, 2)}% of first forms ",
    "with a cause code have a code not in {esrd_cause_mapping_file}; they are ",
    "counted as Other. Check how the codes are written before going on."
  ), call. = FALSE)
}

Facility counts on the dialysis date
This is the step the original code spent most of its length on: it expanded every CMS file to one row per ZIP code per day, over 15 years, to look up counts by date. usRds::add_dialysis_facility_counts() instead finds the CMS release current on each patient’s date of dialysis start and counts facilities in the ZIP code and in its HSA, with an interval join that cannot multiply rows. It also fixes two errors in the original counts (see Differences).


Click to show/hide R Code
# usRds finds the CMS release current on each patient's dialysis start date and
# counts facilities in the patient's ZIP code and in the HSA that ZIP code
# belongs to in that calendar year. This replaces the original code's
# expansion of every facility file to one row per ZIP code per day.
facility_crosswalk &lt;- select(hsa_crosswalk, zip, year, hsanum)

first_forms &lt;- medevid_forms |&gt;
  filter(first_form, has_residence) |&gt;
  select(USRDS_ID, DIALDAT, zip) |&gt;
  add_dialysis_facility_counts(
    date_var = "DIALDAT",
    zip_var = "zip",
    area_crosswalk = facility_crosswalk,
    area_id = "hsanum"
  ) |&gt;
  rename(hsanum_dialysis_date = hsanum) |&gt;
  select(-DIALDAT, -zip)

ACS, HSA and other area data
Each ZIP code is mapped to its ZCTA with the crosswalk for the year dialysis started, and ACS data are joined by ZCTA; the HSA and its population are matched by ZIP code and the same year, so area data and facility counts describe the same year. The original code used the year of the form for area data and silently dropped patients whose two years differed. Every join states its expected relationship, so an unexpected duplicate stops the run.


Click to show/hide R Code
# All area data are matched to the year dialysis started, the same year as the
# facility counts. The original code used the year of the form for these and
# kept only patients whose two years matched (decided 27 October 2026).
linked_forms &lt;- medevid_forms |&gt;
  left_join(first_forms, by = "USRDS_ID", relationship = "many-to-one") |&gt;
  left_join(
    zcta_crosswalk,
    by = join_by(zip, dialysis_year == year),
    relationship = "many-to-one"
  ) |&gt;
  left_join(
    acs_zcta,
    by = join_by(zcta, dialysis_year == year),
    relationship = "many-to-one"
  ) |&gt;
  left_join(
    facility_crosswalk,
    by = join_by(zip, dialysis_year == year),
    relationship = "many-to-one"
  ) |&gt;
  left_join(
    hsa_population,
    by = join_by(dialysis_year == year, hsanum),
    relationship = "many-to-one"
  ) |&gt;
  left_join(hsa_benchmarks, by = "hsanum", relationship = "many-to-one") |&gt;
  left_join(select(ruca_zip, zip, ruca1, rural_urban), by = "zip", relationship = "many-to-one") |&gt;
  left_join(esrd_cause_mapping, by = "PDIS", relationship = "many-to-one") |&gt;
  mutate(
    # Blank codes and codes not in the mapping are "Other", as in the
    # original code (see check-unmapped-causes)
    esrd_cause = factor(coalesce(esrd_cause, "Other"), levels = esrd_cause_categories)
  )

# The joins must not add or drop forms
if (nrow(linked_forms) != nrow(medevid_forms)) {
  stop("Linking area data changed the number of Medical Evidence forms.", call. = FALSE)
}

Regression tables
One table for each published regression table (Table 2 and eTables 1–3), and one for the analysis excluding eGFR of 5 or below, which was in the original R code but not the paper. Each table has one row per model and density term, grouped as the paper groups them. Logistic models give odds ratios; eTable 3 gives differences in eGFR. The formatting function, format_estimates_table(), is shown on the functions page.
Functions used: format_estimates_table().


Click to show/hide R Code
# The estimates from this session if models.R has just run, otherwise the
# saved ones
estimates &lt;- if (exists("model_estimates")) {
  model_estimates
} else {
  readRDS(file.path("models", "model_estimates.rds"))
}

# One gt table per published table (format_estimates_table() in functions.R).
# Logistic models give odds ratios; eTable 3 gives differences in eGFR.
published_titles &lt;- tribble(
  ~table,     ~title,                                                                                   ~estimate_label,
  "Table 2",  "Odds of starting hemodialysis at eGFR ≥ 10 by HSA facility density",                     "OR",
  "eTable 1", "Odds of starting hemodialysis at eGFR ≥ 10, stratified by race",                         "OR",
  "eTable 2", "Odds of starting hemodialysis at eGFR ≥ 10 per unit of HSA facility density",            "OR",
  "eTable 3", "Difference in eGFR at dialysis start by HSA facility density",                           "Difference in eGFR"
)

published_tables &lt;- published_titles |&gt;
  pmap(function(table, title, estimate_label) {
    estimates$published |&gt;
      filter(table == .env$table) |&gt;
      format_estimates_table(title, estimate_label)
  }) |&gt;
  set_names(published_titles$table)

table_egfr_range &lt;- estimates$egfr_range |&gt;
  rename(estimate = odds_ratio) |&gt;
  mutate(block = "Original R code, not in the paper") |&gt;
  format_estimates_table("Model 3 excluding eGFR of 5 or below", "OR")

ZIP code to HSA
One Dartmouth crosswalk per year, 2011 to 2019. The 2018 and 2019 files are published as zipped CSV rather than Excel; the original reader only looked for Excel files. Each ZIP code and year must appear once.
Functions used: standardize_zip(), assert_unique().


Click to show/hide R Code
# One row per ZIP code and year. 2011-2017 files are .xls; 2018-2019 are
# zipped .csv. The ZIP code column is named zipcodeYY in every year.
read_crosswalk &lt;- function(path, year) {
  crosswalk &lt;- if (str_detect(path, "\\.zip$")) {
    read_csv(path, col_types = cols(.default = col_character()))
  } else {
    read_excel(path, col_types = "text")
  }

  crosswalk |&gt;
    rename_with(str_to_lower) |&gt;
    select(zip = starts_with("zipcode"), hsanum, hsastate) |&gt;
    transmute(
      zip = standardize_zip(zip),
      year = as.integer(year),
      hsanum = as.integer(hsanum),
      hsa_state = str_to_upper(hsastate)
    )
}

hsa_crosswalk &lt;- crosswalk_files |&gt;
  imap(read_crosswalk) |&gt;
  list_rbind() |&gt;
  filter(!is.na(zip)) |&gt;
  assert_unique(zip, year)

ZIP code to ZCTA
ACS data are published by ZIP Code Tabulation Area (ZCTA), not by ZIP code. About a fifth of ZIP codes, mostly PO box and large-volume ZIP codes, have no ZCTA of their own and belong to a surrounding ZCTA with a different number. The UDS Mapper crosswalk for each year, bundled in zippeR (Prener et al., n.d.; UDS Mapper, n.d.), maps every ZIP code to its ZCTA. The original analysis intended this step but never applied it; it matched ZIP codes to ZCTAs directly, so residents of those ZIP codes found no ACS data.
Functions used: standardize_zip(), assert_unique().


Click to show/hide R Code
# ZIP code to ZCTA, by year, from the UDS Mapper crosswalks bundled in zippeR.
# About a fifth of ZIP codes (mostly PO box and large-volume ZIP codes) belong
# to a ZCTA with a different number. The original code intended this step but
# never applied it, matching ZIP codes to ZCTAs directly.
zcta_crosswalk &lt;- study_years |&gt;
  map(function(y) {
    zi_load_crosswalk(zip_source = "UDS", year = y) |&gt;
      rename_with(str_to_upper) |&gt;
      transmute(zip = standardize_zip(ZIP), year = as.integer(y), zcta = standardize_zip(ZCTA))
  }) |&gt;
  list_rbind() |&gt;
  filter(!is.na(zip), !is.na(zcta)) |&gt;
  assert_unique(zip, year)

American Community Survey
ZCTA estimates from the 5-year subject table S0601 for each study year. Every year’s variable labels are checked first. The ZCTA code is taken as the last five characters of the Census identifier (GEOID), because tidycensus removes the two-digit state prefix only for 2013 and later (Walker and Herman, n.d.); the key is then checked to be unique by ZCTA and year. The estimates are keyed by ZCTA and joined to patients through the crosswalk above.
Functions used: assert_unique(), check_acs_labels().


Click to show/hide R Code
# ZCTA estimates from ACS 5-year table S0601 for each study year. Each year's
# variable labels are checked first, because subject-table codes can change.
# The ZCTA code is the last five characters of GEOID: tidycensus 2.0.0 strips
# the two-digit state prefix from ZCTA GEOIDs only for 2013 and later, so for
# 2011-2012 GEOID may still begin with the state code.
acs_labels &lt;- study_years |&gt;
  map(function(y) {
    load_variables(y, "acs5/subject", cache = TRUE) |&gt;
      filter(name %in% acs_variables$variable) |&gt;
      transmute(year = y, name, label)
  }) |&gt;
  list_rbind()

check_acs_labels(acs_labels, acs_variables)

acs_zcta &lt;- study_years |&gt;
  map(function(y) {
    get_acs(
      geography = "zcta",
      variables = set_names(acs_variables$variable, acs_variables$name),
      year = y,
      survey = "acs5",
      cache_table = TRUE
    ) |&gt;
      transmute(zcta = str_sub(GEOID, -5), year = y, variable, estimate)
  }) |&gt;
  list_rbind() |&gt;
  pivot_wider(names_from = variable, values_from = estimate) |&gt;
  assert_unique(zcta, year)

Rural-urban type
The 2010 ERS ZIP code file, grouped into metropolitan, micropolitan, small town and rural by primary RUCA code.
Functions used: standardize_zip(), assert_unique().


Click to show/hide R Code
# Primary RUCA code of each ZIP code (2010 file), grouped into four types.
ruca_zip &lt;- read_excel(ruca_file, sheet = "Data", col_types = "text") |&gt;
  rename_with(str_to_upper) |&gt;
  transmute(zip = standardize_zip(ZIP_CODE), ruca1 = as.numeric(RUCA1)) |&gt;
  left_join(ruca_groups, by = "ruca1", relationship = "many-to-one") |&gt;
  mutate(rural_urban = factor(rural_urban, levels = unique(ruca_groups$rural_urban))) |&gt;
  assert_unique(zip)

HSA boundaries
Used only for the maps in Figure 2.


Click to show/hide R Code
# HSA boundaries for maps (figures.R only).
boundary_dir &lt;- file.path(public_data_dir, "hsa_boundaries")
if (!dir.exists(boundary_dir)) {
  unzip(boundary_zip, exdir = boundary_dir)
}

hsa_boundaries &lt;- list.files(boundary_dir, pattern = "\\.shp$", full.names = TRUE, recursive = TRUE) |&gt;
  first() |&gt;
  read_sf() |&gt;
  rename_with(str_to_lower) |&gt;
  transmute(hsanum = as.integer(hsa93)) |&gt;
  st_make_valid()

Figure 1
fc_draw() draws the same flowchart object that produced the cohort.


Click to show/hide R Code
dir.create(figures_dir, showWarnings = FALSE)
cohort_fc |&gt;
  fc_draw(big.mark = ",") |&gt;
  fc_export(
    file.path(figures_dir, "figure_1_cohort_flow.png"),
    width = 3000, height = 4200, res = 300
