# HSA population from ZCTA populations. Several ZIP codes can share one ZCTA,
# so each ZCTA is counted once per HSA and year; summing over ZIP codes would
# count a shared ZCTA's population more than once. ZCTAs without ACS data
# contribute nothing.
hsa_population_from_zcta &lt;- function(hsa_crosswalk, zcta_crosswalk, zcta_population) {
  hsa_crosswalk |&gt;
    select(zip, year, hsanum) |&gt;
    inner_join(zcta_crosswalk, by = join_by(zip, year), relationship = "one-to-one") |&gt;
    distinct(year, hsanum, zcta) |&gt;
    left_join(zcta_population, by = join_by(zcta, year), relationship = "many-to-one") |&gt;
    summarize(hsa_population = sum(zip_population, na.rm = TRUE), .by = c(year, hsanum))
}

Exclusions
The steps, in order, with the reason for each. Steps 1 to 9 define the patients the paper studies; steps 10 to 17 keep patients whose area data can be linked, as the original code did. The analysis plan, section 4, has the details.

First Medical Evidence form per patient. Later forms describe a second or later episode of dialysis, not its start.
Dialysis started 2011 to 2019, after the IDEAL trial changed practice (see Clinical background).
Age 18 or older.
Hemodialysis, not peritoneal dialysis.
In-center dialysis. The timing of a home dialysis start depends on training schedules and trainer availability, and can be very early by design, so the paper excludes them (Hemmige et al. 2024).
Insurance recorded, a Model 3 covariate.
Access type recorded, a Model 2 covariate.
Sex recorded, a Model 1 covariate.
eGFR recorded and 20 or below, the range in which nephrologists decide when to start (see The eGFR thresholds).
A residential ZIP code on the date dialysis started, to place the patient in an area.
The ZIP code has a ZCTA in that year’s crosswalk, the Census area for which ACS data are published.
ACS data for that ZCTA and year.
Facility data for the date dialysis started, to count facilities.
The ZIP code is in a Dartmouth HSA, the unit of the exposure.
The 48 contiguous states or the District of Columbia, as the paper studies the continental US (Hemmige et al. 2024).
Race and Hispanic ethnicity recorded, Model 1 covariates.
Complete ACS and rural-urban data for the ZIP code, Model 3 covariates. ZIP codes with RUCA code 99 have no population.

Steps 11 and 12 are one step in the plan. Each covariate step removes patients so that every model uses the same cohort, as in the original code; only the albumin and hemoglobin models use fewer patients, and they report their own numbers.
In the original code the cohort was filtered in about 27 separate places, and a hand-maintained table recorded the counts. The table and the data drifted apart: one exclusion was recorded as removing nobody while the next line removed patients; at least eight filters (including the facility linkage) were never recorded; two rows were labelled with the wrong variable; and one recorded step was never applied. Here each step both filters and counts, so the diagram cannot disagree with the data.


